.TH OPEN 2
.SH NAME
open \- open for reading or writing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.PP
.B int open(const char *name, int mode);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Open
opens the file
.I name
for reading
(if
.I mode
is 0),
writing (if
.I mode
is 1) or for both reading and writing
(if
.I mode
is 2).
.B Note:
<fcntl.h> names those three O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY and O_RDWR.  v7 had no such
header and its page wrote the digits.
.I Name
is the address of a string of ASCII characters representing
a path name, terminated by a null character.
.PP
The file is positioned at the beginning (byte 0).
The returned file descriptor must be used for subsequent calls
for other input-output functions on the file.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
creat(2), read(2), write(2), dup(2), close(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The value \-1 is returned
if the file does not exist,
if one of the necessary directories
does not exist or is unreadable, if the file is not
readable (resp. writable), or if too many files are open.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_open
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
